    "claims": [
      {
        "text": "Real GDP increased at an annual rate of 2.7 percent in Q4 2022, compared with 3.2 percent in Q3 2022 — a slowdown of 0.5 percentage points.",
        "kind": "model_interpretation",
        "citations": [
          {
            "source_id": "source_26300000000000000000000000000002",
            "location": {
              "character_start": 412,
              "character_end": 663,
              "page": 1
            },
            "quotation_match": "whitespace_normalized",
            "support_status": "not_independently_verified",
            "quotation": "Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 2.7 percent in the fourth quarter of\n2022 (table 1), according to the \"second\" estimate released by the Bureau of Economic Analysis. In the\nthird quarter, real GDP increased 3.2 percent."
          }
        ],
        "support_status": "not_independently_verified",
        "role": "support",
        "uncited_percentage_points": [
          "0.5 percentage points"
        ],
        "uncited_quarters": [
          "Q3 2022"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Both goods and services consumption did NOT increase in Q4 2022. Services spending increased, but goods spending decreased (services were partly offset by a decrease in goods), with the leading goods decline in 'other' durable goods (mainly jewelry).",
        "kind": "model_interpretation",
        "citations": [
          {
            "source_id": "source_26300000000000000000000000000002",
            "location": {
              "character_start": 2149,
              "character_end": 2448,
              "page": 2
            },
            "quotation_match": "whitespace_normalized",
            "support_status": "not_independently_verified",
            "quotation": "The increase in\nconsumer spending reflected an increase in services that was partly offset by a decrease in goods.\nWithin services, the increase was led by health care as well as housing and utilities. Within goods, the\nleading contributor to the decrease was \"other\" durable goods (mainly jewelry)."
          }
        ],
        "support_status": "not_independently_verified",
        "role": "support",
        "uncited_quarters": [
          "Q4 2022"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The 2.7 percent Q4 figure is a downward revision of an estimate (from the 2.9 percent advance estimate) based on more complete source data, not an actual change in economic activity.",
        "kind": "model_interpretation",
        "citations": [
          {
            "source_id": "source_26300000000000000000000000000002",
            "location": {
              "character_start": 665,
              "character_end": 1030,
              "page": 1
            },
            "quotation_match": "whitespace_normalized",
            "support_status": "not_independently_verified",
            "quotation": "The GDP estimate released today is based on more complete source data than were available for the\n\"advance\" estimate issued last month. In the advance estimate, the increase in real GDP was 2.9\npercent. The updated estimates primarily reflected a downward revision to consumer spending that was\npartly offset by an upward revision to nonresidential fixed investment"
          }
        ],
        "support_status": "not_independently_verified",
        "role": "support",
